{"lines": ["Now the most famous code in the world, sitting in a courtyard at the CIA. Kryptos, by the sculptor Jim Sanborn, dedicated on the third of November, 1990. A curved copper screen, with four coded messages.", "Three of them fell. The first public solver was a computer scientist, Jim Gillogly, in 1999. The CIA later said one of its own analysts, David Stein, had solved them in 1998, with pencil and paper.", "The fourth, called K4, has ninety-seven characters. It has never been publicly solved.", "Then Sanborn started giving clues. In 2010, he revealed that six letters decrypt to BERLIN. In 2014, five more spelled CLOCK. Four clues in total.", "And still, nobody has publicly solved it. A clue narrows the search. It does not finish it.", "In 2025, Sanborn put his archive, with the answer to K4, up for auction. On the twentieth of November, it sold for nine hundred and sixty-two thousand, five hundred dollars. In June 2026, the buyer, a firm called Paradigm, stepped forward. As far as I can find, the solution is still not published.", "Rule four. A clue is not an answer. Prove the whole message."], "words": [["Now", 0.0, 0.16], ["the", 0.16, 0.36], ["most", 0.36, 0.54], ["famous", 0.54, 0.88], ["code", 0.88, 1.32], ["in", 1.32, 1.58], ["the", 1.58, 1.66], ["world,", 1.66, 1.94], ["sitting", 1.94, 2.52], ["in", 2.52, 2.76], ["a", 2.76, 2.82], ["courtyard", 2.82, 3.16], ["at", 3.16, 3.64], ["the", 3.64, 3.78], ["CIA.", 3.78, 4.08], ["Kryptos,", 4.08, 5.52], ["by", 5.52, 6.08], ["the", 6.08, 6.2], ["sculptor", 6.2, 6.68], ["Jim", 6.68, 6.9], ["Sanborn,", 6.9, 7.52], ["dedicated", 7.52, 8.14], ["on", 8.14, 8.74], ["the", 8.74, 8.8], ["third", 8.8, 9.22], ["of", 9.22, 9.28], ["November,", 9.28, 9.66], ["1990.", 9.66, 10.34], ["A", 12.1, 12.2], ["curved", 12.2, 12.48], ["copper", 12.48, 12.9], ["screen,", 12.9, 13.44], ["with", 13.44, 14.08], ["four", 14.08, 14.38], ["coded", 14.38, 14.84], ["messages.", 14.84, 15.14], ["Three", 16.34, 16.54], ["of", 16.54, 16.66], [